From: InterGenOS <noreply@intergenos.example>
Date: Mon, 25 May 2026
Subject: [PATCH] gtkpopover: skip set_input_region when width/height degenerate

In gtk_popover_update_shape()'s !has_arrow branch, the input rectangle
width is computed as:

    input_rect.width = gdk_surface_get_width(priv->surface) -
                       (shadow_width.left + shadow_width.right);

shadow_width comes from CSS box-shadow extents (typically 24-32px on
modern GNOME themes). During the transient configure handshake on
Wayland — before the compositor has ACKed the xdg_popup's final size
— gdk_surface_get_width() can return values smaller than
shadow_width.left+shadow_width.right, producing a NEGATIVE
cairo_rectangle_int_t.width.

The negative width is passed to cairo_region_create_rectangle() and
pixman_region32_init_rect(), where pixman's BAD_RECT macro fires and
logs:

    *** BUG ***
    In pixman_region32_init_rect: Invalid rectangle passed
    Set a breakpoint on '_pixman_log_error' to debug

Pixman returns an EMPTY region. We then call
gdk_surface_set_input_region(priv->surface, empty_region), which on
the Wayland backend emits wl_surface.set_input_region(empty_region).
Per the Wayland protocol, an empty bounded input region means the
surface accepts ZERO pointer-button or touch events. Motion events
still arrive (the compositor delivers those before the input-region
test for cursor/hover), but every button-press lands nowhere.

On the InterGenOS GNOME 49 live-ISO autologin session, this race hits
with ~100% reliability — and the symptom presents as "every libadwaita
popover-using widget kills clicks on the parent window after dismissal"
plus "window titlebar drag silently no-ops on every Wayland window."
GDK_BACKEND=x11 (XWayland) bypasses both because XWayland uses X11
server-side grabs, not xdg_popup grabs.

Fix: clamp width/height to MAX(0, ...) to eliminate pixman log noise,
and skip set_input_region entirely when the result is non-positive.
The Wayland default for a surface that never calls set_input_region is
"infinite" (NULL), which is the safe behavior during the transient
configure handshake — input events flow normally until a valid size
can be set.

Discovered + diagnosed by the InterGenOS team's research dossier at
docs/research/2026-05-25-gnome49-wayland-regression/.

To be filed upstream as a GitLab issue + MR against gnome-49 + main.

--- a/gtk/gtkpopover.c
+++ b/gtk/gtkpopover.c
@@ -1499,16 +1499,26 @@

       input_rect.x = shadow_width.left;
       input_rect.y = shadow_width.top;
-      input_rect.width =
+      input_rect.width = MAX (0,
         gdk_surface_get_width (priv->surface) -
-        (shadow_width.left + shadow_width.right);
-      input_rect.height =
+        (shadow_width.left + shadow_width.right));
+      input_rect.height = MAX (0,
         gdk_surface_get_height (priv->surface) -
-        (shadow_width.top + shadow_width.bottom);
+        (shadow_width.top + shadow_width.bottom));

-      region = cairo_region_create_rectangle (&input_rect);
-      gdk_surface_set_input_region (priv->surface, region);
-      cairo_region_destroy (region);
+      /* Skip set_input_region when the surface is transiently degenerate
+       * (width or height non-positive). Leaving the input region unset
+       * keeps the Wayland default (infinite) so events flow normally
+       * until the configure handshake settles. Fixes a pixman BAD_RECT
+       * fingerprint that produced an empty input region on the live ISO,
+       * silently breaking pointer-button delivery on libadwaita popover-
+       * using widgets. See InterGenOS 2026-05-25 research dossier. */
+      if (input_rect.width > 0 && input_rect.height > 0)
+        {
+          region = cairo_region_create_rectangle (&input_rect);
+          gdk_surface_set_input_region (priv->surface, region);
+          cairo_region_destroy (region);
+        }
     }
 }
